 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What's going on in the body",
   "text": "A paper reports that MPS IIID is caused by a missing or broken gene called GNS [E4d28745]. This gene normally makes an enzyme that lives inside a part of the cell called a lysosome, which a paper reports is deficient in MPS IIID [Ef8f5811]. A paper reports that without enough of this enzyme, a sugar chain called heparan sulfate builds up in the body [E9f300a9]. A paper reports that another substance called glycosphingolipid also builds up in the brain [E4576c7c]. A paper reports that this buildup is part of why nerve cells are lost over time [Ed294171].",
   "citations": [
    "E4d28745",
    "Ef8f5811",
    "E9f300a9",
    "E4576c7c",
    "Ed294171"
   ],
   "status": "ok"
  },
  {
   "key": "similar",
   "title": "Diseases that share the same biology",
   "text": "MPS IIIA, MPS IIIB, MPS IIIC, MPS I (Hurler) and MPS II (Hunter) all involve an enzyme inside the lysosome not working, the same kind of problem seen in MPS IIID [Ef8f5811, E8282821, E8a7d8c7, E108a4c3, E834d07e, Ef5d5a50]. A paper reports that each of these conditions also involves a buildup of heparan sulfate inside cells, the same substance that builds up in MPS IIID [E9f300a9, Ec586fdd, Eae4e3d8, E7373ae4, Ea90608d, E1f3fa46]. According to HPO, MPS IIIA happens because of a different gene called SGSH [E6659f43]. According to Reactome, MPS IIID, MPS IIIA, MPS IIIB, MPS IIIC, MPS I and MPS II are all grouped together in the same pathway family called Mucopolysaccharidoses [Ea3f8b18, E472e813, Ef52839f, Ed1d9a61, Eb6e6b47, E1ba2695].",
